// An outcome kept as a value: the success of an operation that throws, or the error it threw.
// A function that can fail declares `throws E`, and its caller handles the error at the call with `try`, `try?` or
// `catch`. That leaves nothing to store, which is right for nearly every call. `Result` is for the rest: an outcome
// that has to outlive the call, travel through a container or a channel, or be decided later. It is an ordinary
// variant with no compiler privileges, so it is built, matched and asked about the way any variant is:
//   let stored: Result<int, DigitError> = try .Success(ReadDigit(c)) catch { e => .Failure(e) };
//   if stored is .Success(_) { ... }
// Asking which case a result holds with `is` borrows it rather than consuming it, so a result whose payload owns
// something can be asked and then matched.

/// The outcome of an operation that can fail, kept as a value: what it produced, or the error it threw.
/// Owns its payload, so a `Result` is move-only exactly when its success or error type is.
/// @typeParam T what a success holds
/// @typeParam E what a failure holds
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/result
pub variant Result<T, E> {
    /// The operation succeeded and produced this value.
    Success(T),
    /// The operation failed with this error.
    Failure(E)
}
